%h1 Instructor Guide: Specifying Due Dates
%p
  CodeWorkout uses a YAML-based interface for managing workout availability and student extensions. This allows you to quickly set up schedules for multiple sections and handle individual student needs in a single, human-readable view.

%h2 1. Workout Offering Dates
%p
  Each section of a course has three primary date fields:
%ul
  %li
    %b due:
    The main due date (soft deadline). Students can still submit after this date if a hard deadline is set.
  %li
    %b from:
    The opening date. The workout becomes visible and accessible at this time. Use 
    %code always
    if you want it available immediately.
  %li
    %b until:
    The hard deadline. Submissions are completely blocked after this time. If not specified, it defaults to 
    %code +0 minutes
    (meaning the workout closes exactly at the due date).

%h2 2. Relative Offsets
%p
  Instead of entering full date/time strings, you can use relative offsets from the 
  %code due
  date. This is especially useful for setting up consistent schedules across sections.
%ul
  %li
    %b Flexible Units:
    You can use 
    %code minutes
    (or 
    %code min
    ), 
    %code hours
    (or 
    %code hr
    ), 
    %code days
    (or 
    %code d
    ), or 
    %code weeks
    (or 
    %code w
    ).
  %li
    %b Opening Dates (from):
    These must be negative. You can use a leading 
    %code -
    sign (e.g., 
    %code -3 days
    ) or no sign at all (e.g., 
    %code 1 week
    will be interpreted as 
    %code -1 week
    ).
  %li
    %b Hard Deadlines (until):
    These must be non-negative. You can use a leading 
    %code +
    sign (e.g., 
    %code +12 hours
    ) or no sign at all (e.g., 
    %code 1 day
    will be interpreted as 
    %code +1 day
    ).

%h3 Example Configuration:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    sections:
      - section: CS 101 Section A
        due: 2025-09-15 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week           # Opens 1 week before due date
        until: +2 days         # Closes 2 days after due date
      - section: CS 101 Section B
        due: 2025-09-16 11:59 PM
        from: always           # Always open
        until: +0 minutes      # Closes exactly at due date

%h2 3. Student Extensions
%p
  Student extensions allow you to override the default dates for specific students. You can group multiple students who share the same extension dates.
%ul
  %li
    Extensions use the same 
    %code due
    , 
    %code from
    , and 
    %code until
    rules as regular offerings.
  %li
    Students can be specified by their full name and email (e.g., 
    %code John Doe <jdoe@example.edu>
    ) or just their email.

%h3 Example Extensions:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    extensions:
      - due: 2025-09-20 11:59 PM
        from: always
        until: +0 minutes
        students:
          - Alice Smith <asmith@example.edu>
          - Bob Jones <bjones@example.edu>
      - due: +3 days           # 3 days after their original section due date
        from: -1 week
        until: +1 day
        students:
          - charlie@example.edu

%h2 4. Time Limit Overrides (Extensions)
%p
  Student extensions allow you to override the workout's time duration limit for specific students (e.g., for SSD accommodations or individual adjustments) using the
  %code time_limit:
  setting within an extension group.
%ul
  %li
    %b Minutes:
    Specify exact minutes such as
    %code 90
    ,
    %code 90 minutes
    , or
    %code 90 min
    \.
  %li
    %b Hours:
    Specify hours such as
    %code 1.5 hours
    ,
    %code 2 hrs
    , or
    %code 1 hour
    (automatically converted to minutes).
  %li
    %b Multipliers:
    Specify a multiplier relative to the workout's base time limit, such as
    %code 1.5x
    or
    %code 2x
    (e.g.,
    %code 1.5x
    on a workout with a 60-minute base time limit automatically computes a 90-minute limit).
  %li
    %b Unlimited:
    Specify
    %code unlimited
    or
    %code none
    to completely lift the time limit for those students.
  %li
    %b Aliases:
    You can also use
    %code time:
    or
    %code duration:
    interchangeably with
    %code time_limit:
    \.

%h3 Example with Time Limit Overrides:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    extensions:
      - time_limit: 1.5x                # 1.5x accommodation on base time limit
        students:
          - Alice Smith <asmith@example.edu>
      - due: 2025-09-20 11:59 PM
        time_limit: 90 minutes          # Overrides time limit to 90 minutes
        students:
          - Bob Jones <bjones@example.edu>
      - time_limit: unlimited           # No time limit for Charlie
        students:
          - charlie@example.edu

%h2 5. IP Access Restrictions
%p
  You can restrict workout access to specific network locations (such as on-campus labs or test centers) using the
  %code ips:
  setting in the YAML.
%ul
  %li
    %b Global Default:
    Specify
    %code ips:
    at the top level to apply to all sections by default.
  %li
    %b Per-Section Override:
    Specify
    %code ips:
    inside a specific section to override the default for that section.
  %li
    %b Student Extension Override:
    Specify
    %code ips:
    within an extension group to allow specific students to access from different IPs or use
    %code any
    to completely remove all IP restrictions for those students.
  %li
    %b Supported Formats:
    Single IPs (e.g.
    %code 192.168.1.50
    ,
    %code ::1
    ), CIDR notation (e.g.
    %code 192.168.1.0/24
    ,
    %code 10.0.0.0/8
    ), wildcards (e.g.
    %code 128.173.*.*
    ), or comma-separated lists. Use
    %code any
    to permit access from anywhere.

%h3 Example with IP Restrictions:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    ips: 128.173.*.*, 198.82.0.0/16     # Default for all sections
    sections:
      - section: CS 101 Section A
        due: 2025-09-15 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week
        until: +2 days
      - section: CS 101 Lab B
        due: 2025-09-16 11:59 PM
        from: always
        until: +0 minutes
        ips: 192.168.1.0/24             # Overrides default for Lab B

    extensions:
      - ips: any                        # Removes IP restrictions for Alice
        students:
          - Alice Smith <asmith@example.edu>

%h2 6. Browser Agent Requirements
%p
  You can require students to use specific secure testing browsers (such as Respondus LockDown Browser or Secure Exam Browser) using the
  %code browsers:
  setting in the YAML.
%ul
  %li
    %b Global Default:
    Specify
    %code browsers:
    at the top level to require the browser for all sections by default.
  %li
    %b Per-Section Override:
    Specify
    %code browsers:
    inside a section to set or override the requirement for that section.
  %li
    %b Student Extension Override:
    Specify
    %code browsers:
    in an extension group to allow specific students to use a different browser or specify
    %code any
    to completely lift browser restrictions for those students.
  %li
    %b Supported Formats:
    Browser substrings (e.g.
    %code LockDown Browser
    ,
    %code SEB
    ), wildcards (e.g.
    %code *LockDown*
    ), or comma-separated lists. Use
    %code any
    to allow any standard browser.

%h3 Example with Browser Requirements:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    browsers: LockDown Browser, SEB     # Require LockDown Browser or SEB for all sections
    sections:
      - section: CS 101 Section A
        due: 2025-09-15 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week
        until: +2 days
      - section: CS 101 Practice Lab
        due: 2025-09-16 11:59 PM
        from: always
        until: +0 minutes
        browsers: any                   # No browser requirement for practice lab

    extensions:
      - browsers: any                   # Removes browser requirement for Alice
        students:
          - Alice Smith <asmith@example.edu>

%h2 7. Workout and Exercise Scoring Policies
%p
  You can allow students to take the workout multiple times and control how their final grades and individual problem scores are recorded using
  %code workout_attempts:
  ,
  %code workout_score_to_keep:
  , and
  %code exercise_score_to_keep:
  in the YAML.
%ul
  %li
    %b workout_attempts:
    The maximum number of full workout attempts allowed per student (e.g.,
    %code 2
    or
    %code 3
    ). If omitted, it defaults to 1 attempt. You can also specify
    %code 0
    or
    %code unlimited
    for unlimited retakes.
  %li
    %b Time Limit Requirement:
    Whenever multiple workout attempts are allowed (attempt limit greater than 1 or unlimited), a
    %code time_limit
    is required so students cannot take unlimited time across repeated attempts.
  %li
    %b workout_score_to_keep:
    Controls which workout attempt is used for the overall workout grade:
    %code highest
    (default) uses the best score across all attempts, while
    %code latest
    uses the score from the most recently completed attempt.
  %li
    %b exercise_score_to_keep:
    Controls which submission score is kept for each individual exercise within an attempt:
    %code latest
    (default) records the score of their latest submission on that question, while
    %code highest
    records the highest score earned across all submissions on that question.
  %li
    %b Global Defaults:
    Specify
    %code workout_attempts:
    ,
    %code workout_score_to_keep:
    , or
    %code exercise_score_to_keep:
    at the top level to apply to all sections by default.
  %li
    %b Per-Section Override:
    Specify
    %code workout_attempts:
    ,
    %code workout_score_to_keep:
    , or
    %code exercise_score_to_keep:
    inside a specific section to customize scoring for that section.
  %li
    %b Student Extension Override:
    Specify
    %code workout_attempts:
    within an extension group to grant additional retakes to individual students (e.g., for makeup exams or special circumstances).

%h3 Example with Workout & Exercise Scoring:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    workout_attempts: 2                 # Allow 2 workout attempts for all sections
    workout_score_to_keep: highest      # Keep highest score among workout attempts
    exercise_score_to_keep: highest     # Keep highest score on each exercise
    sections:
      - section: CS 101 Section A
        due: 2025-09-15 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week
        until: +2 days
      - section: CS 101 Honors
        due: 2025-09-15 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week
        until: +0 minutes
        workout_attempts: 1             # Only 1 workout attempt for honors
        exercise_score_to_keep: latest  # Exercise score based on latest attempt

    extensions:
      - workout_attempts: 3             # 1 extra makeup attempt for Alice
        students:
          - Alice Smith <asmith@example.edu>

%h2 8. LMS Assignment URLs
%p
  You can associate course offerings with their corresponding assignment in Canvas, Blackboard, or other Learning Management Systems using the
  %code lms_assignment_url:
  setting in the YAML.
%ul
  %li
    %b Global Default:
    Specify
    %code lms_assignment_url:
    (or alias
    %code url:
    ) at the top level if all sections share the same assignment or if there is a single primary LMS link.
  %li
    %b Per-Section Override:
    If different sections have different assignment IDs or URLs in your LMS, specify
    %code lms_assignment_url:
    inside each section under
    %code sections:
    \.

%h3 Example with LMS URLs:
%pre.examples
  :preserve
    lms_assignment_url: https://canvas.example.edu/courses/101/assignments/1001
    sections:
      - section: CS 101 Section A
        due: 2025-09-15 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week
        until: +2 days
      - section: CS 101 Section B
        due: 2025-09-16 11:59 PM
        from: 1 week
        until: +2 days
        lms_assignment_url: https://canvas.example.edu/courses/101/assignments/1002

%h2 9. Tips for Success
%ul
  %li
    %b Time Zones:
    All absolute dates are interpreted in your current time zone (as set in your profile).
  %li
    %b Validation:
    The system will notify you if you enter an invalid student, an impossible relative offset (like a positive
    %code from
    date), or multiple attempts without a required time limit.
  %li
    %b Human Readable:
    When you save the form, the system will clean up your YAML to ensure it uses standardized units and explicit signs for clarity.

